## 5. Cross-Functional Collaboration Patterns

### MLE ↔ Data Scientists (Research Handoff)

The collaboration follows a structured hypothesis-to-production pipeline:

1. **DS develops hypotheses** from data analysis and system performance
2. **DS selects and validates** a hypothesis as pilot
3. **MLE collaborates on implementation** — discusses feasibility, adjusts algorithms, modifies pipelines
4. **Joint A/B testing and validation** — DS provides ongoing analysis; MLE implements
5. **Review and documentation** — both teams evaluate, document process and lessons learned

Key principles:
- DS experiments in notebooks → MLE converts experimentation code into repeatable scripts/jobs
- DS owns analytical work output (data quality, model design decisions, analytical communication)
- MLE owns production-readiness (scalable, reliable, monitored)
- "MLE works directly with data scientists to convert experimentation code into repeatable scripts/jobs" (Microsoft MLOps Level 3)

[DS Support for MLE Hypothesis Testing](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/CSMAI-1443)
[MLOps Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/VAPPDEV/pages/2166657676/Machine+Learning+Operations+MLOps+Framework)

### MLE ↔ Product Managers (Metric Alignment)

- PM sets product strategy and priorities for the community/mission
- MLE/DS builds roadmap of work to support strategy, working collaboratively with the product squad
- PM aligns on "North Star" metrics and success criteria before model development begins
- Joint metric definition ensures ML outputs tie to business value
- PM provides business context for experiment prioritization

[Working with aligned Product Data Scientists](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/JPA/pages/2220548721/Working+with+aligned+Product+Data+Scientists)

### MLE ↔ SREs (Reliability)

- **SRE defines SLI/SLOs** for model serving endpoints (availability, latency p99, error budgets)
- **MLE provides model-specific runbooks** and operational context
- **Shared on-call for model incidents** — model failures first triaged by MLE on-call, infrastructure failures escalate to SRE
- **Joint capacity planning** — load testing, autoscaling verification, blast-radius assessment
- **Policy authoring** — SREs author rollout gates, cost guardrails, escalation boundaries as version-controlled policies
- **Post-incident collaboration** — PIR generation, SLO concession audits, action item tracking

[Agentic Operations in the New SDLC](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6885869718/Agentic+Operations+in+the+New+SDLC)

### MLE ↔ Data Engineers (Pipeline SLAs)

- **Data Engineers** manage data pipelines, maintain ETL jobs, ensure data freshness
- **MLE** defines feature requirements and transformation logic
- **Joint contracts** via RACI docs to assign responsibility for query logic, update responsibility, historical data storage
- **Pipeline SLAs**: Agreed response times, on-call escalation through `#help-data-engineering` channels
- **Ideal ratio**: 10DS/MLE : 1 Data Engineer (desired at Atlassian)
- **Domain specialization model**: DEs have SME mapping for specific dataset coverage

[MAT Leadership - Data Engineering Collaboration](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~447487496/pages/1900107900/MAT+Leadership+offsite+-+FY23+August+-+Data+engineering+collaboration)

### MLE ↔ ML Platform Team (Infrastructure)

- **ML Platform** provides tooling: ML Studio, MSP, Feature Store, Model Registry
- **MLE** uses platform tools and provides feedback on pain points
- **Service desk model**: `#help-ml-platform` channel for issues, ML Desk tickets for deployment requests
- **Deployment coordination**: MLEs request capacity changes via tickets, actioned by deployment owners or platform owners

[Launchpad and Model Deployment Agility](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AO2/pages/6812865165/Launchpad+and+Model+Deployment+Agility)

