## 4. Common ML Engineering Challenges & Failure Modes

### 4.1 Data & Feature Challenges

From [Growth Reco ML Observability](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~82492541/pages/4424181036/Growth+Reco+ML+Observability+Overview), [Interpreting ML Observability](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/KYC/pages/4486104906/Interpreting+ML+Observability+Results), and [Model drift identification](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CA3/pages/1953707277/Model+drift+identification+and+mitigation+strategies):

| Challenge | Description | Mitigation Strategy |
|-----------|-------------|---------------------|
| **Feature/Data Drift** | Distribution of model input features changes over time (e.g., new customer demographics, product feature changes) | Statistical tests (KS, Chi-squared, PSI) comparing current vs. reference distributions; automated alerting |
| **Concept Drift** | Relationship between inputs and outputs changes (e.g., user preference shifts) | Monitor model output quality metrics; set up automated retraining triggers |
| **Training-Serving Skew** | Feature values differ between training (offline) and serving (online) | Unified feature transformation layer via feature store (Tecton/MLFS); point-in-time correctness |
| **Feature Quality Degradation** | Upstream data sources change schemas, go stale, or produce unexpected nulls | Data quality SDK with schema validation, null rate checks, freshness monitoring |
| **Label Drift** | Changes in labeling criteria or upstream label generation logic | Label distribution monitoring; automated alerts when proportions shift |

### 4.2 Training & Model Challenges

From [Phase 0.2:10x Faster HSTU Training](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020d90617ddcbda4dd992fbd2ae3e7ff2da/pages/6424140357/Phase+0.2+10x+Faster+HSTU+Training+50M+scale) and [CI/CD for MLOps](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~6359788e1cc605b1fd1664ec/pages/6726837663/CI+CD+for+MLOps):

| Challenge | Description | Mitigation Strategy |
|-----------|-------------|---------------------|
| **Training Instability** | Learning rate collapse, vanishing updates (update_ratio → 0), gradient explosion | Monitor update_ratio_global; tune min_lr, warmup_steps, weight_decay; implement gradient clipping |
| **GPU Utilization Collapse** | Pipeline bottlenecks starving GPU of data (15% utilization observed) | Profile before optimizing; pre-tensorize data; remove Spark from training loop; tune DataLoader workers/prefetch |
| **OOM Errors** | Model/batch too large for available GPU memory | Chunked data reads; batch size tuning; PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF; gradient checkpointing |
| **Reproducibility Issues** | Training runs inconsistent, experiments hard to compare | MLflow experiment tracking; automated logging; deterministic seeds; version-controlled configs |
| **DDP Failures** | Distributed training crashes (unused parameters, metric desync, CUDA re-init) | Freeze unused modules; explicit cross-rank metric reduction; separate post-fit evaluation |

### 4.3 Serving & Production Challenges

From [Current Pain Points with Model Serving via SageMaker](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020cd3f787132154dc3b533d4df685f7759/pages/5350721817/Current+Pain+Points+with+Model+Serving+via+Sagemaker), [ML Latency Optimization](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020ff70b14e5dca488b8ea018f735980b62/pages/4087104885/ML+Latency+Optimization+Findings):

| Challenge | Description | Mitigation Strategy |
|-----------|-------------|---------------------|
| **Serving Latency** | Model inference too slow for real-time use cases (observed: 600ms → target: <200ms) |ONNX/TensorRT conversion (72% reduction); gRPC over HTTP; mixed precision; Flash Attention; quantization |
| **Model Degradation** | Performance drops over time without code changes | Continuous monitoring with ML Lens; metric-based retraining triggers; data drift alerts |
| **Autoscaling Lag** | SageMaker takes 5-10 min to autoscale | Pre-warm instances; traffic-driven prediction scaling; ASG floor preservation |
| **Deployment Failures** | Silent failures during model updates (stuck stacks, rollback failures) | Safe-rollback wrapper scripts; canary deployments; health-check decoupling; blue-green deployments |
| **Cost Overprovisioning** | GPU resources over-allocated due to misconfiguration | Regular capacity audits; right-sizing; multi-model serving on shared GPU; inference component architecture |

### 4.4 Industry Statistics on ML Production Failures

From [ML Pipeline CI/CD Validation Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~6359788e1cc605b1fd1664ec/pages/6723101866/ML+Pipeline+CI+CD+Validation+Framework+core-ml-batch-pipelines):

> "Industry post-mortems consistently show **most ML production incidents are caused by data failures, not model failures** — things that CI/CD validation directly prevents."

