## 5. MLE Tech Stacks & Architectural Patterns

### 5.1 Atlassian ML Platform Architecture

From [Atlassian AI Infrastructure & Platform Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120208fca6f07a4e3413ea2ec03c396573bb7/pages/5831803039/Atlassian+AI+Infrastructure+Platform+Strategy):

The platform is organized into **three vertical pillars**:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    ML PLATFORM ARCHITECTURE                   │
├───────────────────┬───────────────────┬─────────────────────┤
│     ML DATA│   ML TRAINING│    ML SERVING       │
├───────────────────┼───────────────────┼─────────────────────┤
│ • Data generation │ • Experiment mgmt │ • Real-time infer.│
│ • Feature eng.    │ • Distributed│ • Batch inference   │
│ • Dataset mgmt    │   training│ • Model optimization│
│ • Privacy/        │ • Evaluation      │ • Responsible AI│
│   compliance      │ • Model optim.    │   guardrails        │
└───────────────────┴───────────────────┴─────────────────────┘
```

**Guiding Principles:**
1. Unified & Flexible Platform (AWS, GCP; PyTorch, HuggingFace, JAX; GPU, TPU)
2. Performance & Efficiency (elastic compute, automated scaling)
3. Developer Velocity (self-service, automation, standardized APIs)
4. Responsible & Secure AI
5. Continuous Learning & Collaboration
6. LLM-First, Not LLM-Only

### 5.2 Specific Tech Stack Components

From [Architecture of AI/ML feature](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~bnagy/pages/4420377390/Architecture+of+AI+ML+feature), [MLOps Architecture](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/1904300165/MLOps+Architecture), and various internal docs:

| Layer | Technology | Purpose |
|-------|-----------|---------|
| **Feature Store** | Tecton → MLFS (migration), DynamoDB (online), S3/Delta Lake (offline) | Feature engineering, storage, and serving; batch/stream/on-demand features |
| **Training Platform** | ML Studio (Databricks), ML Fabric, MosaicML, KITT Compute | Experiment orchestration, workflow management, GPU cluster scheduling |
| **Experiment Tracking** | MLflow (integrated into ML Studio) | Parameter/metric logging, model versioning, experiment comparison |
| **Model Registry** | Tarot (model registry), MLflow Model Registry | Model versioning, staging, promotion gates |
| **Model Serving** | TEAMServe (NVIDIA Triton/TensorRT-LLM/vLLM), SageMaker (MSP),ONNX sidecar | Real-time & batch inference, multi-region, GPU-backed K8s |
| **Orchestration** | Airflow, ML Studio Workflow Orchestrator, Rundeck | Pipeline scheduling, nested workflows, continuous training |
| **Data Processing** | Apache Spark/PySpark, Databricks, PyArrow | Distributed data processing, feature computation |
| **Monitoring** | ML Lens (observability), SignalFx, Splunk,EvidentlyAI | Drift detection, metric tracking, alerting |
| **Experimentation** | Statsig (A/B testing), Switcheroo (feature flags) | Online experiments, traffic splitting, metric scorecards |
| **Infrastructure** | AWS (SageMaker, EC2 GPU), GCP, KITT (K8s), Docker | Compute resources, autoscaling, multi-region deployment |

### 5.3 ML Pipeline Architecture Pattern (CI/CD)

From [ML Pipeline CI/CD Validation Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~6359788e1cc605b1fd1664ec/pages/6723101866/ML+Pipeline+CI+CD+Validation+Framework+core-ml-batch-pipelines):

```
Code/Data Push → PR Checks → Merge → Train Model → Evaluate Model→ Quality Gates → Register in MLflow → Deploy to Staging
    → Integration Tests → Deploy to Production → Scheduled Monitoring→ Drift Detection → Auto-Retrain (if threshold breached)
```

**Three-Stage Pipeline:**
1. **On Every PR**: Lint, type check, unit tests, vulnerability scan, data schema validation
2. **On Merge to Main**: Train model, evaluate, quality gate, register, deploy staging, integration test, deploy prod
3. **Scheduled (Daily/Weekly)**: Drift monitoring, auto-retrain trigger, performance report

### 5.4 Model Serving Deployment Patterns

From [ML model serving deployment patterns](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~lnagy/pages/2198980072/ML+model+serving+deployment+patterns+with+Pythia) and [Model Serving Sidecar](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/KNOWLEDGE/pages/6657380646/Model+Serving+Sidecar+Implementation+Plan):

| Pattern | Description | Best For |
|---------|-------------|----------|
| **SageMaker + Lambda** | Model endpoint behind API GW, Lambda fetches features from feature store | Low-traffic, event-driven |
| **SageMaker + Sidecar** | Consuming service runs prediction sidecar that fetches features and calls model endpoint | Moderate latency tolerance |
| **ONNX Sidecar** | Deploy sidecar next to consumer; Pythia fetches features and executes model locally | Low-latency CPU inference |
| **TEAMServe (Triton/vLLM)** | K8s-based GPU inference with multi-framework support | High-throughput, real-time LLM/DL |
| **FastAPI Model Serving Sidecar** | Quick experimentation, shadow traffic, A/B testing at low volume | Prototyping, validation |

### 5.5 Feature Store Architecture

From [Tecton to MLFS Migration Roadmap](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6068686994/Tecton+to+MLFS+Migration+Roadmap) and [MLFS Feature Serving](https://home.atlassian.com/o/2346a038-3c8c-498b-a79b-e7847859868d/s/a436116f-02ce-4520-8fbb-7301462a1674/project/ATLAS-90351):

Key architectural requirements:
- **Dual Storage**: Online (DynamoDB/Redis for<100ms P99) + Offline (S3/Delta Lake for batch training)
- **Point-in-Time Correctness**: Historical feature retrieval matching observation timestamps
- **Training-Serving Consistency**: Same transformation logic for both training and inference
- **Feature Types**: Batch (historical aggregations), Stream (near-real-time events), On-Demand (request-time computations)
- **Multi-Region Support**: Tenant-based routing, regional replication

