## 6. Domain Expertise Requirements & Specializations

### 6.1 Primary MLE Specializations at Atlassian

| Domain | Key Applications | Required Expertise |
|--------|-----------------|-------------------|
| **Search & Ranking** | L1/L2 rankers, query rewriting, passage reranking | NDCG, MRR, BM25, neural ranking models, interleaving experiments |
| **Recommendation Systems** | Template recommendations, content recommendations, next-best-play | Contextual bandits, collaborative filtering, wide & deep networks, HSTU |
| **NLP & LLMs** | Query understanding, text classification, entity linking, content labeling | Transformers, fine-tuning (LoRA/QLoRA), prompt engineering, RAG |
| **Forecasting** | Revenue forecasting, capacity planning | Time series models (Prophet), seasonality detection, ensemble methods |
| **Anomaly Detection** | Canary analysis, security detection, incident detection | LSTM, autoencoders, statistical outlier detection |
| **Growth & Engagement** | User propensity modeling, churn prediction, upsell modeling | XGBoost/LightGBM, feature importance, SHAP explainability |

### 6.2 Classical ML vs. Deep Learning Decision Framework

From internal production examples:

| Use When | Classical ML (XGBoost, LightGBM) | Deep Learning (PyTorch, Transformers) |
|----------|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------|
| **Data volume** | Thousands–millions of tabular rows | Millions+ of unstructured/sequential data |
| **Interpretability** | SHAP, feature importance needed | Black-box acceptable |
| **Latency constraints** | <10ms inference required | Can tolerate 50ms–500ms+ |
| **Feature engineering** | Manual, domain-driven features | Learned representations |
| **Infrastructure cost** | CPU-only serving possible | GPU required for training and often serving |
| **Production examples** | Jira Issue Classifier (10ms), Early Warning System (LightGBM), Next Best Play | L2 Ranker (neural), Query Rewrite (Llama 3.1 8B), HSTU (transformer-based rec) |

### 6.3 Business Acumen Requirements

- **80/20 Rule**: Focus on business practicality; "having something now" vs. "perfection sometime in the future" (stated in multiple Atlassian MLE JDs)
- **Cost-Performance Tradeoffs**: Understanding when a simpler model at1/100th the cost is preferable (e.g., Jira classifier reduced from $500K/year SageMaker cost by scaling to 5% when no active use case)
- **Metric Alignment**: Connecting ML metrics (precision, recall, NDCG) to business outcomes (engagement, retention, revenue)
- **Experiment Design**: Understanding statistical significance, sample size requirements, experiment duration

### 6.4 Soft Skills & Competencies

From the [Phase 1 Deep-Dive](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TADT/pages/7026569052/Phase+1+Deep-Dive+Machine+Learning+Engineer+MLE):

- **Collaboration**: Cross-functional collaboration, stakeholder management, end-to-end ownership
- **Leadership**: Mentorship, technical leadership, strong decision-making under ambiguity
- **Communication**: Explain data science concepts to diverse audiences; craft compelling stories with data; research communication and publishing mindset

