## 7. MLOps Maturity Model & Continuous Training

### 7.1 MLOps Maturity Levels

From [PMP ML Framework Proposal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CT5/pages/6427288801/PMP+ML+Framework+Proposal+Accelerating+Experimentation+Automation+and+the+Path+to+MLOps+Level+1):

| Level | Characteristics | Atlassian Status |
|-------|----------------|-----------------|
| **Level 0** | Manual, script-driven; no CI/CD for ML; no automated monitoring | Many teams still here |
| **Level 1** | ML pipeline automation; continuous training triggered by data; experiment tracking | Target for most teams |
| **Level 2** | CI/CD for pipelines; automated testing/validation; automated retraining on drift | Aspirational for mature teams |

### 7.2 Continuous Training Architecture

From [MLOps Architecture](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/1904300165/MLOps+Architecture) and [ML Studio Continuous Model Training](https://home.atlassian.com/o/2346a038-3c8c-498b-a79b-e7847859868d/s/a436116f-02ce-4520-8fbb-7301462a1674/project/ATLAS-69956):

Key insight: Unlike traditional DevOps where changes are always human-initiated, MLOps introduces **data-triggered changes** — the serving model may be updated without any code change, triggered by new training data or drift detection signals.

Components:
1. **ML Pipeline**: Reads training data → transforms → trains → validates → registers in model registry
2. **Model Serving CI/CD**: Takes serving definition → validates → deploys to staging/production
3. **Monitoring System**: Captures production metrics → feeds back to data lake → can trigger retraining
4. **Model Registry (Tarot/MLflow)**: Acts as the bridge between training and deployment workflows

---

## 8. Key Findings & Patterns for AI MLE Agent Design

### 8.1 What an MLE Agent Would Need to Be Grounded In

Based on this research, an autonomous MLE agent would need expertise across:

1. **Data pipeline operations**: PySpark transformations, feature extraction, data quality validation, schema checks
2. **Feature store interaction**: Tecton/MLFS API for feature registration, serving configuration, point-in-time joins
3. **Training orchestration**: ML Studio workflow creation, hyperparameter tuning (Hyperopt/Optuna), distributed training setup (DDP)
4. **Experiment management**: MLflow API for logging, comparison, model registration
5. **Evaluation frameworks**: Offline metrics (precision, recall, NDCG, MAP), online experiment design (A/B, interleaving, shadow)
6. **Deployment patterns**: SageMaker/TEAMServe deployment, canary rollouts, traffic splitting via Statsig
7. **Monitoring & observability**: ML Lens integration, drift detection (KS test, PSI), alerting configuration
8. **CI/CD for ML**: Quality gates, automated validation, rollback strategies
9. **Optimization**: Mixed precision, quantization, TensorRT compilation, batch size tuning, GPU utilization profiling

### 8.2 Critical Gaps an Agent Must Handle

- **Data quality is the #1 production failure mode** — more incidents from bad data than bad models
- **Training-serving skew** is the most subtle and dangerous bug class in ML systems
- **Cost awareness** is essential — GPU resources are expensive, right-sizing matters
- **Leveling is the #1 hiring blocker** — a well-calibrated MLE agent needs to operate at the right sophistication level for its target tasks

